### 3. Network Architecture Paradigms and Perimeter Segmentation

#### Context & Strategic Importance

Prescriptive network architecture places defensive controls intentionally across physical and logical topologies, rather than relying on default flat connectivity. Network engineering aligns topology design with threat intelligence and business requirements to control traffic flow, restrict lateral movement by adversaries, and enforce access policy boundaries.

#### On-Premises and Hybrid Network Models

Traditional physical on-premises network architectures rely on hardware routers, switches, and perimeter firewalls connected via physical media. Traffic control in physical architectures is enforced through static Access Control Lists (ACLs) and stateful firewall rule tables programmed directly into individual hardware devices. While proven, pure physical architectures are operationally rigid, requiring manual configuration changes on individual hardware nodes to adapt to dynamic enterprise traffic demands.

Modern enterprise environments deploy hybrid network models. These architectures integrate on-premises physical hardware with software-defined wide area networks, virtualized platforms, virtual private clouds (VPCs), and serverless application environments. A hybrid design enables physical workstations, on-premises server racks, remote Virtual Private Network (VPN) users, VPC subnets, and serverless applications to operate under unified security governance.

#### Segmentation Strategies & Zero Trust Framework

Network segmentation divides enterprise topologies into smaller, isolated subnets to constrain traffic flows, prevent sensitive data spillage, and block lateral adversary movement.

Segmentation can be implemented physically or logically across the protocol stack:

- **Physical Segmentation:** Uses dedicated physical hardware interfaces on separate switches and routers to isolate network subnets.
- **Logical Segmentation:** Isolates endpoints independent of physical location using Virtual Local Area Networks (VLANs), Software-Defined Networking (SDN) overlay tunnels, and end-to-end network encryption. Logical segmentation operates primarily at the Internet layer of the TCP/IP model (equivalent to the Network layer of the OSI model).

