|  |    +-------------------------------------------------------------+    |  |
|  |    | Subnet / Host Segment A  <-- Continuous Verification -->    |    |  |
|  |    | Subnet / Host Segment B  <-- Identity / Context Checks -->  |    |  |
|  |    | Subnet / Host Segment C  <-- Policy Enforcement Engine -->  |    |  |
|  |    +-------------------------------------------------------------+    |  |
|  +-----------------------------------------------------------------------+  |
|  * Rule: Default Untrusted Status for ALL entities. Re-verification required.|  |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
```

Network segmentation provides the foundation for **Zero Trust Architecture**. The core tenet of Zero Trust is that no entity—whether an internal host, remote user, application process, or incoming packet—is trusted by default, regardless of network location. Even after an initial authentication event grants access, Zero Trust requires continuous identity and policy re-verification. This model assumes that endpoints may become compromised post-authentication, preventing attackers from pivoting laterally across internal segments.

**Architectural Note & Exam Highlight:** Network segmentation is primarily deployed to safeguard sensitive data by isolating high-value assets. Hosts can be segmented physically (dedicated interfaces/switches) or logically (VLANs and cryptographic tunnels). Under Zero Trust frameworks, continuous re-verification is required post-authentication to mitigate risks from host compromise.

#### Next-Generation Networking Paradigms

Modern networking decouples control plane decision-making from infrastructure plane data forwarding:

- **Software-Defined Networking (SDN):** Decouples control logic from hardware packet processing.
    - _Control Layer (Control Plane):_ Centralized software controllers manage routing topologies, network services, and policy enforcement dynamically.
    - _Infrastructure Layer (Data Plane):_ Physical or virtual switches perform high-speed packet forwarding based on instructions received from the control layer.
- **Secure Access Secure Edge (SASE):** Integrates Software-Defined Wide Area Networking (SD-WAN) with cloud-delivered Zero Trust security mechanisms into a unified framework. SASE delivers identity-based access controls, web gateway security, and threat protection directly from the cloud. SASE enforces security policies consistently across globally distributed users, devices, and applications regardless of physical location.

**Architectural Note & Exam Highlight:** Secure Access Secure Edge (SASE) combines Software-Defined Wide Area Networking (SD-WAN) with Zero Trust security controls delivered via cloud-native architectures.

