- **Type-1 (Bare-Metal) Hypervisors:** Installed directly on physical server hardware without an intermediate host operating system. They manage guest operating systems with direct, low-latency access to hardware. Examples include VMware ESXi, Microsoft Hyper-V, and KVM.
- **Type-2 (Hosted) Hypervisors:** Run as software applications within an existing host operating system. The host OS abstracts hardware access for guest operating systems. Examples include VMware Workstation, Oracle VM VirtualBox, and Parallels Desktop.
- **Containerization:** Operates at the OS kernel level rather than abstracting hardware. Containers package application binaries, libraries, and configuration files into an isolated sandbox sharing the host OS kernel. Containers launch rapidly and consume minimal overhead compared to full Virtual Machines (VMs).
- **Serverless Architecture:** Leverages containerization and virtualization abstractions to execute microcode application functions (Function as a Service / FaaS) without requiring customers to provision or manage server infrastructure. Scaling occurs dynamically, and billing is calculated solely during active execution cycles. Because security teams lack access to underlying host operating systems in serverless models, security controls must be implemented directly at the application code and function level.

**Architectural Note & Exam Highlight:** Serverless architectures leverage underlying virtualization and containerization to execute discrete microcode functions in abstract environments, shifting security enforcement directly to application code and API identity controls.

