|  | - Monitors USB transfers, local storage, clipboard paste operations     |  |
|  | - Direct communication with central DLP Policy Server                 |  |
|  +-----------------------------------------------------------------------+  |
|                                                                             |
|  [ Network DLP (NDLP) ]                                                     |
|  +-----------------------------------------------------------------------+  |
|  | Gateway / Egress Network Appliance                                    |  |
|  | - Inspects unencrypted outbound network streams                        |  |
|  | - Performs pattern checks against data in transit                     |  |
|  +-----------------------------------------------------------------------+  |
|                                                                             |
|  [ Hybrid DLP Model ]                                                       |
|  +-----------------------------------------------------------------------+  |
|  | Combines EDLP local agent visibility with NDLP gateway inspection     |  |
|  | to provide full coverage across internal devices and network boundaries|  |
|  +-----------------------------------------------------------------------+  |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
```

- **Endpoint DLP (EDLP):** Installs software agents directly on end-user workstations and servers. The EDLP agent inspects data at the point of creation or local processing (e.g., entering PII into a local form, copying data to removable media, or printing files). EDLP provides visibility into local device operations that network-based inspection cannot observe.
- **Network DLP (NDLP):** Deploys dedicated gateway hardware appliances at network perimeters to inspect outbound network traffic, analyzing protocols and file transfers for sensitive data patterns.
- **Hybrid DLP:** Combines EDLP and NDLP implementations to cover both local device actions and network transport vectors. While Hybrid DLP is the most complex and costly model to deploy, it provides the most comprehensive data protection coverage.

To inspect encrypted network flows for sensitive data leakage, organizations deploy **SSL/TLS Inspection**. Although legacy Secure Sockets Layer (SSL) protocols have been deprecated due to severe vulnerabilities and replaced by Transport Layer Security (TLS), industry and examination terminology continues to refer to cryptographic traffic decryption as _SSL inspection_.

SSL/TLS inspection proxies break certificate-based network encryption on egress traffic, decrypting encrypted payloads, performing DLP pattern analysis and content classification, and re-encrypting the traffic before transmitting it to its destination. Decrypting outbound streams ensures that malicious actors and unauthorized processes cannot use TLS encryption to exfiltrate sensitive enterprise data undetected.

